{% block content %}
  <main>
    <article class="article">
      <h1 class="section-title">7 running lessons from Jakob Ingebrigtsen</h1>
      <p class="article-meta"><svg style="width:1em;height:1em;vertical-align:-0.15em;margin-right:0.3em" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M16 2v4M8 2v4M3 10h18"/></svg>18 June 2026 · <span class="accent"><svg style="width:1em;height:1em;vertical-align:-0.15em;margin-right:0.25em" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 2l2.9 6.26 6.88.56-5.2 4.52 1.56 6.7L12 17.27 5.86 20.54l1.56-6.7-5.2-4.52 6.88-.56z"/></svg>Champions</span> · 5 min read</p>

      <picture>
        <source type="image/avif" srcset="images/cat-champions.avif?v=1">
        <source type="image/webp" srcset="images/cat-champions.webp?v=1">
        <img src="images/cat-champions.jpg?v=1" alt="Running shoe laces: a symbol of dedication to running" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="740" height="494">
      </picture>
      <p>
        When you watch <strong>Jakob Ingebrigtsen</strong> run, you'd think everything comes easy.
        The Norwegian has Olympic gold in the 1500m (Tokyo) and the 5000m (Paris), and he grew up
        in a family of runners: his older brothers, Henrik and Filip, also raced at international
        level.
      </p>
      <p>
        In interviews he often says things that fit every runner, not just champions. I gathered 7
        of them.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Trust the process</h3>
      <p style="font-style:italic; color:var(--text); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); padding-left:1rem;">
        "You can't pull the carrot out of the ground to see if it's growing."
      </p>
      <p>
        With this wonderful analogy he explains that you don't need to constantly test your limits.
        Improvement is slow and cumulative. In practice: don't run a 5K test every week, and don't
        get discouraged if you don't see immediate results. Progress comes after months of
        consistency.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Consistency matters more than talent</h3>
      <p style="font-style:italic; color:var(--text); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); padding-left:1rem;">
        "You don't need to be fast from the start. If you train with consistency and dedication, year after year you'll get better."
      </p>
      <p>
        The 10K in 40 minutes can wait. Get out regularly, build habits and learn to enjoy the
        process. Talent helps, but consistency is what lasts.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Don't try to be a hero in training</h3>
      <p style="font-style:italic; color:var(--text); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); padding-left:1rem;">
        "Save your best for race day."
      </p>
      <p>
        Ingebrigtsen keeps most of his sessions controlled, at the right intensity, without
        overdoing it and without ego. The all-or-nothing effort is saved for race day.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Recovery is part of training</h3>
      <p>
        Your body adapts and gets stronger while you rest. Without enough recovery, the hard work
        doesn't "sink in", and overdoing it often leads to injury.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Motivation isn't always necessary</h3>
      <p style="font-style:italic; color:var(--text); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); padding-left:1rem;">
        "I don't feel motivated or unmotivated. It's irrelevant."
      </p>
      <p>
        He doesn't wait to feel like it before running. He runs because he has decided this is his
        path. And here lies a big secret: <strong>great athletes rely on their habits, not on
        motivation.</strong>
      </p>

      <h3>6. Don't fear failure</h3>
      <p style="font-style:italic; color:var(--text); border-left:4px solid var(--accent); padding-left:1rem;">
        "You can't be afraid of the consequences of failure. You have to keep an open mind and be ready for the challenges."
      </p>
      <p>
        Every runner will at some point lose a race, get injured, have bad days, doubt themselves.
        All of that is part of the road, and the progress continues.
      </p>

      <h3>7. Aim for progress, not perfection</h3>
      <p>
        Beyond the medals, Jakob wants to get better every year. Continuous, small improvement is
        his driving force, and it's something any of us can adopt.
      </p>
      <!-- YOUR OWN LINE: Which of the 7 speaks to you most, and why? Two or three sentences,
           e.g. if you used to "test" yourself every week or got injured from doing too much. -->

      <p>
        The next time you hesitate to go for a run, remember the carrot. You don't need to pull it
        out of the soil. Just tie your laces and let it grow.
      </p>

